<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chp-if-switch-statements" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>If and Switch Statements</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In the previous chapter, you learned to organize MATLAB commands into scripts and
            functions. While a script runs top to bottom without deviation, most real programs
            need to make decisions: take one action when a condition is true, and a different
            action when it is false. This chapter introduces the two primary decision-making
            tools in MATLAB: <term>if statements</term> and <term>switch statements</term>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Before writing if statements, you need to understand the values they test.
            This chapter builds from the ground up:
            <dl width="narrow">
                <li><title>Logical Values</title>
                    <p>The building blocks of decisions — <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf> (stored as 1 and 0).</p>
                </li>
                <li><title>Logical Statements</title>
                    <p>Comparisons and compound conditions formed with operators like <pf>==</pf>, <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf>, and <pf>||</pf>.</p>
                </li>
                <li><title>If Statements</title>
                    <p>Conditional execution using <pf>if</pf>, <pf>elseif</pf>, and <pf>else</pf>.</p>
                </li>
                <li><title>Switch Statements</title>
                    <p>Cleaner multi-way branching when selecting among exact values.</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <p>
            By the end of this chapter you will be able to evaluate logical expressions,
            write if/elseif/else chains, and choose between if and switch based on the problem at hand.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <xi:include href="sec-logical-values.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="sec-logical-statements.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="sec-if-statements.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="sec-switch-statements.ptx" />
    <conclusion>
        <title>Key Takeaways</title>
        <objectives xml:id="takeaways-if-switch-statements">
            <ul>
                <li><p>Logical values are stored as <pf>1</pf> (true) or <pf>0</pf> (false) in MATLAB.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use relational operators (<pf>==</pf>, <pf>~=</pf>, <pf>&lt;</pf>, <pf>&gt;</pf>, <pf>&lt;=</pf>, <pf>&gt;=</pf>) to compare values and produce logical results.</p></li>
                <li><p>Combine conditions with <pf>&amp;&amp;</pf> (and), <pf>||</pf> (or), and <pf>~</pf> (not).</p></li>
                <li><p>An if statement executes a block only when its condition is true; <pf>elseif</pf> and <pf>else</pf> handle additional and default cases.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use a switch statement instead of a long if-elseif chain when branching on the exact value of a single variable.</p></li>
                <li><p>Every <pf>if</pf> and <pf>switch</pf> block must be closed with <pf>end</pf>.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </conclusion>
</chapter>
